<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Malcolm X</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1925">1925</a>–<a href="/years/1965">1965</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Malcolm Little, el-Hajj Malik el-Shabazz</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Malcolm X was an African American Muslim minister and civil rights leader. Born Malcolm Little, he joined the Nation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in prison and from 1952 was its public face, urging Black empowerment and separation from white Americans and criticising the mainstream civil rights movement's emphasis on non-violence and integration. He left the Nation in 1964, made the pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, embraced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni Islam</a> and founded the Organization of Afro-American Unity. He was assassinated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> in February 1965; his ideas were an important influence on the Black power movement.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/malcolm-x.jpg" alt="Malcolm X" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marion S. Trikosko · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Malcolm_X_in_1964.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
